Project: smsh

Purpose: A small command interpreter that reads commands interactively
         or from a script and runs built-in and external commands.

Outline:
	smsh displays a prompt and reads one command line at a time. It
        splits each line into whitespace-delimited arguments, processes
        variable and inline-command substitutions, and dispatches the
        command. Built-ins manage shell state; other commands run in a
        child process. The shell also supports basic if/then/else/fi
        conditional blocks, script files, and a -c command mode.

Output:
	Interactive input is preceded by the > prompt. Script and -c input
        do not display a prompt. Built-ins and external programs write
        their output to the shell's standard output and standard error.
        The set command lists stored variables, marking exported values.
        Inline command substitution captures command output and inserts
        it into the command line, replacing newlines with spaces.

Arguments:
	smsh accepts no arguments for interactive use. With -c, it reads
        the command assembled from the remaining arguments. Otherwise,
        the first argument is treated as a script file; later arguments
        are made available to the script as positional variables named
        1, 2, and so on. The shell supports the built-ins set, export,
        cd, exit, and read, as well as NAME=value assignments. export
        marks a variable for inclusion in the environment of child
        processes. cd changes the shell's working directory; exit uses
        an optional numeric status or the last command result.

Input:
        Commands are read one line at a time from standard input, a
        script file, or the temporary command stream used by -c. Lines
        are split on spaces and tabs. The shell recognizes $NAME, $$,
        and $? substitutions, backslash escapes, comments beginning
        with an unescaped #, and inline commands written as $(command).
        Quoting, pipelines, and input/output redirection are not
        implemented by the shell.

Data Structures:
        FLEXSTR:  a dynamically growing character buffer used to read
                  command lines.
        FLEXLIST: a dynamically growing list of separately allocated
                  argument strings, returned as a NULL-terminated argv.
        struct var: stores a name=value string, whether it is exported,
                    and whether it is a private script variable. The
                    variable table has a fixed capacity of 200 entries.
        Control-flow state: static state records whether an if command
                            is awaiting then or executing a then/else
                            block, and stores the condition result.

Memory Usage:
	Command lines, argument lists, substitution results, and flexible
        strings are dynamically allocated and released after use.
        Variable values are stored in the fixed-size variable table;
        replacing a value frees its previous string. Child processes
        receive a separately allocated environment pointer array, while
        the strings it references remain owned by the variable table.

Program Structure/Layering:
        main
        |-- setup                      [load environment; set signals]
        |-- setup_env                  [interactive, -c, or script input]
        `-- do_smsh
            |-- next_cmd               [read a command line]
            `-- splitline
                |-- varsub             [variables, escapes, comments]
                |-- execute_inline_commands
                |   `-- execute_inline_command
                |       `-- execute   [capture command output]
                `-- process
                    |-- do_control_command  [if, then, else, fi]
                    |   `-- process        [run the if condition]
                    `-- do_command          [when execution is allowed]
                        |-- is_builtin
                        |   |-- assignments, set, export, cd, exit, read
                        |   `-- variable helpers in varlib.c
                        `-- execute         [fork, build environment,
                                             execvp, and wait]

Source Files:
	smsh.c:       initializes the shell, selects input mode, and runs
                      the command loop
        process.c:    dispatches control-flow commands and commands;
                      starts external programs and captures inline output
        process.h:    declarations for command-processing functions
        builtin.c:    implements built-in commands, assignments, comments,
                      variable substitution, and inline-command parsing
        builtin.h:    declarations for built-in and substitution functions
        controlflow.c: tracks and processes if, then, else, and fi state
        controlflow.h: declarations for control-flow functions
        varlib.c:     stores shell variables and builds child environments
        varlib.h:     declarations for variable-table functions
        splitline2.c: reads command lines, tokenizes arguments, and frees lists
        splitline2.h: declarations for command-reading and parsing functions
        flexstr2.c:   dynamically growing string and string-list helpers
        flexstr2.h:   flexible string/list structures and declarations
        smsh.h:       shared shell declarations
        Makefile:     builds smsh; make clean removes objects and executable

Files:
        The shell reads a script file when one is supplied. In -c mode,
        it writes the command arguments to a temporary stream before
        processing them. Inline command substitution creates and unlinks
        a temporary file to capture command output. Other file access is
        performed by commands started by the shell, not by the shell's
        command parser.

Error Handling:
	Failure to open a script or create a temporary stream is reported
        and causes the shell to exit. Allocation failures in the string
        and list helpers are reported through fatal. A failed fork, wait,
        or exec is reported by the command-execution layer. Built-ins
        return failure for invalid or missing arguments and failed
        operations; conditional syntax errors are reported and reset the
        control-flow state. The shell stores the most recent processing
        result for use by $? and exit.


